ev('EVD118',"Sky checks for Ch.30 and App. B (analysis/ch30_31_sky_checks.py). (a) Swanson, Bangor, 22 Apr 1998 2122 PDT, looking west: Sun -12, no Moon, Venus, Jupiter, Mars or Saturn above the horizon; brightest western stars were Capella (alt 41, WNW), Pollux, Procyon, Betelgeuse; nothing astronomical explains a 100-ft disc moving slowly at low altitude. I found no fireball record for 22 Apr 1998 (search found none). (b) Junkin, Poulsbo, 16 Sep 2004 2100 PDT: Arcturus (m-0.05) was at altitude ~21 deg due west, the brightest object in the west; Moon (8%) and planets below the horizon: a scintillating low star is a candidate for the 'dancing, colour-changing' light, though Junkin says he compared it with nearby stars. (c) Fernandina Beach, 23 Jun 2001 2145 EDT: a ~9% crescent Moon was at altitude 13 deg in the WNW, consistent with the witness's own 'fingernail crescent setting in the west' (a check supporting the account's date/time); Mars (m-2.4) stood at 20 deg in the SE (az 141), not 'due east'; Altair (m0.8) was ~13 deg due east. The object itself is not astronomical. (d) Palo Verde, 20 Jul 2004 2215 MST: Jupiter and the 13% Moon had just set in the west (az ~280), whereas the lights were SW: no match. (e) Indian Point, 24 Jun 1984 2230 EDT: Jupiter (m-2.5) alt 15 SE, Mars and Saturn in the south; date uncertain (SRC065). (f) Chernobyl, 26 Apr 1986 ~0425 MSD: Moon (97%) alt ~14 in the SSW and Mars (m-0.5) alt ~9 in the SE, both low and likely orange, are candidates for a 'brass-coloured ball'. (g) Yankee Atomic, 13 Sep 1967 2300 EDT: Moon (80%) alt 18 SSW, Saturn alt 33 ESE; the '6-7 lights over the plant' are not accounted for. Time/direction from witnesses are approximate in all cases.",'ANALYST_CALCULATION','analysis/ch30_31_sky_checks.py','INC090;INC091;INC092;INC093;INC094;INC095;INC096;EXP056;EXP057;EXP058','22343','MEDIUM','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD119',"Appendix B and Ch.30 evidence structure: every item is a NUFORC posting, a researcher's synopsis, or a single interview; none is investigated (Hastings concedes NUFORC lacks capacity); Indian Point's date differs from other sources (14 Jun and 24 Jul 1984), and the Chernobyl accounts are a 1994 MUFON paper and a 2002 Pravda retrospective, not contemporary records. Several Ch.30 NUFORC entries contain internal inconsistencies (e.g., the 17 Jul 2000 report starts at 22:50 but has the helicopter leaving at about 21:30). Hastings treats absence of a Navy response as secrecy and, separately, the Navy's FOIA 'knew nothing' answer as evidence of concealment; neither is documentary.",'ANALYST_ASSESSMENT','book Ch.30 and App. B; SRC065','INC090;INC091;INC092;INC093;INC094;INC095;INC096;INC097','22314','HIGH','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
ev('EVD120',"Ch.31 (conclusion): states the author's inference that UFOs are extraterrestrial and responsible for the book's nuclear incidents. Taken against what this project has verified (see EVD005-EVD117 and the incident statuses), the documentary record supports: that real shutdowns occurred (Echo, 1967) with an official cause undetermined; that Minot 1968 had real radar and B-52 evidence with an incompletely explained residue; that SAC and the CIA/AF treated UFO reports as security matters. It does not support: any link between UFOs and the Echo/Oscar shutdowns in the contemporaneous record, an extraterrestrial inference, or the claim that radar data alone prove craft beyond known aircraft (the Minot speeds were revised, and several radar cases are explained as anomalous propagation). The claim that no witness was ever visited by agents (CLM215) sits uneasily with the book's own accounts of OSI debriefings, PRP threats and NDAs (e.g., CLM039, CLM127, CLM149); it is not testable here.",'ANALYST_ASSESSMENT','project database','CLM213;CLM214;CLM215','22937','CRITICAL','PARTIALLY_VERIFIED')
